Route a failed assertion through a host handler, not printf

CONFIG_DEBUG_ASSERT ships enabled, so patch 0007's handler is what runs on a
broken invariant -- and it printed to stdout before aborting. A sandboxed
FSKit extension has no stdout anyone can read, so the one message that says
*why* the driver just aborted was written where it could never be seen.

Point ext4_assert_failed at an extern ext4b_assert_fail() the shim provides.
The shim logs through the bridge logger (os_log in the appex, stderr in the
tool) and then abort()s. The decision to abort is unchanged and deliberate: a
filesystem that keeps running past a failed invariant writes corruption with
authority, so fail-stop is correct; this only fixes where the reason is
reported. Every binary linking the core defines the handler.

diff --git a/include/ext4_debug.h b/include/ext4_debug.h
--- a/include/ext4_debug.h
+++ b/include/ext4_debug.h
@@ -176,14 +176,20 @@
  * Ending the process instead tears the mount down, gives callers EIO, and
  * leaves a crash report naming the invariant that broke.
  *
+ * The message goes through a host-provided handler rather than printf: a
+ * sandboxed FSKit extension has no stdout anyone can read, so the reason for
+ * the abort was invisible exactly where it mattered most. ext4b_assert_fail
+ * (defined by the shim) routes it to os_log and then abort()s -- still
+ * fail-stop, now with a record. It does not return.
+ *
  * This lives in a function rather than in the macro body because ext4_journal.c
  * has parameters named `abort`, which shadow the libc function at the point of
  * expansion. */
+void ext4b_assert_fail(const char *file, int line);
+
 static inline void ext4_assert_failed(const char *file, int line)
 {
-	printf("assertion failed:\nfile: %s\nline: %d\n", file, line);
-	fflush(stdout);
-	abort();
+	ext4b_assert_fail(file, line);
 }
 
 #define ext4_assert(_v)                                                        \
